---
/**
 * Methodology hub. Each dataset's own caveats live with the dataset; this page
 * covers the decisions that apply across all of them.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  field="sources"
  title="Methodology"
  description="How the data is fetched, processed, checked and published, and the judgement calls behind it."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Methodology</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Decisions that apply to everything here</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          Per-dataset caveats live with the dataset, in the popover attached to every figure and
          on the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>. Terms are defined in the
          <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>. This page covers the choices that apply
          across all of them.
        </p>

        <h2>Our own indexes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="/methodology/governance-readiness/">Governance Readiness Index</a>: the
            scoring rubric, what it does and does not measure, and how the EU is handled.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/policy/australia/#about-this-index">AI Law and Policy Index</a>: how
            instruments are selected and how "how binding is this" is decided.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Comparing things that are not comparable</h2>
        <p>
          Several pages set two measures against each other. Where the measures have different
          units, they are converted to percentile ranks within the set of entities present in both
          before being compared. A use rate of 40 percent and a readiness score of 40 are not the
          same kind of 40, and subtracting one from the other directly would produce a number with
          no meaning.
        </p>
        <p>
          The consequence, stated wherever it applies: the picture moves when the set of entities
          changes. A rank is a statement about relative standing, which is all a cross-unit
          comparison can honestly be.
        </p>

        <h2>Trends</h2>
        <p>
          Where a growth rate is fitted, it is ordinary least squares on the logarithm of the
          quantity against time, over a window that is stated. It describes what has already
          happened and is deliberately not extended past the last observation. This site publishes
          no forecasts of its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fits are checked against alternative windows and the result of that check is printed in
          the figure's own note, so a reader can see whether the answer depends on where the
          window starts.
        </p>

        <h2>Missing data</h2>
        <p>
          A suppressed or absent value is never treated as zero. An entity with no data is left
          unshaded on a map and absent from a table, and the count of what is missing is stated
          next to the count of what is present. Where a join drops entities, they are named.
        </p>
        <p>
          This matters more than it sounds. Reported compute clusters, reported incidents and
          reported adoption all measure reporting as much as they measure the thing. An absence of
          incidents in a country usually means an absence of reporting.
        </p>

        <h2>Freshness</h2>
        <p>
          Every figure carries the date its data was retrieved, and anything older than 45 days is
          marked stale rather than presented as current. When a source cannot be fetched, the
          last cached copy is served and its age is shown: stale data with a visible date beats a
          page that vanishes.
        </p>
        <p>
          A dataset is only rewritten when its records change, so the repository history
          distinguishes a day when data moved from a day when the pipeline merely ran.
        </p>

        <h2>Classification</h2>
        <p>
          News items are categorised by keyword rule, and every item on the
          <a href="/news/">news pages</a> prints the terms that matched. The rule is blunt: it
          misses things and occasionally files something oddly. Publishing the terms is what makes
          it arguable rather than authoritative.
        </p>

        <h2>Checks</h2>
        <p>
          Each stage carries a runnable check, and the ones that matter are verified by being
          deliberately broken to confirm they fail. The licence guard is checked against sources
          it must block. The news classifier is checked against real headlines it previously got
          wrong. The map's coordinate decoder is checked against a hand-computed example and
          against the mistake that would otherwise be made silently.
        </p>
        <p>
          That last habit came from a real incident: a bulk edit once rewrote a normalisation rule
          and the assertion guarding it into a matching pair that passed while doing nothing. A
          check that still passes when the logic is broken is worse than no check.
        </p>

        <h2>Typography</h2>
        <p>
          Extracts from feeds have their dash characters normalised to plain hyphens, which is a
          house style rule rather than an editorial one. It changes presentation and not meaning,
          and the unaltered original is one link away from every extract.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
